--- (4) CANDIDATE LAWS (weighted LS in linear Phi; weights 1/SE^2) ---
    fit points: SPARC bins (non-empty, SE>0) + O01 primary + O01 deep tail, N = 14
    (a) Phi = c x^p :            c = 1.494 +- 0.079,  p = +0.086 +- 0.028  (z_p = +3.12),  chi2 = 94.3/12 = 7.86
    (b) Phi = 1 + alpha ln x :   c0 = 1.492 +- 0.074,  alpha = +0.116 +- 0.037  (z_alpha = +3.16),  chi2 = 94.0/12 = 7.84
    (c) Phi = (1+x)/(1+x/b) :    b = 3.990 +- 1.078  (family implies Phi(1) = 1.599; the constraint Phi(1)=1 forces b=1, the constant Phi=1, degenerate), chi2 = 116.9/13 = 8.99
    (c2) Phi = c_b(1+x)/(1+x/b), Phi(1)=1:  b = 0.602 +- 0.038  (deep Phi(0) = 1.330, Phi(inf) = 0.801),  chi2 = 132.0/13 = 10.16

--- (5) VERDICT (pre-registered rule) ---
    C1: 66.7% of 9 overlapping comparisons within 3 combined SE (need >= 2/3): PASS
    C2: power-law exponent p/SE_p = +3.12 (need |z| > 5): FAIL
    VERDICT: The sign-mirror is a GENUINE SYSTEMATICS DIVIDE (registered finding): Phi(x) does NOT collapse across surveys at fixed x -- the same conclusion N05's arbitration program would reach, now data-side.
    BEST FIT: form (b);  Phi(x) = 1 +0.116 ln x  [alpha = +0.116 +- 0.037, chi2/dof = 7.84; fit on SPARC bins + O01 dwarfs].  Falsifier: any survey with >= 3 rings in one x-bin whose mean Phi departs > 3 combined SE from this curve; or a flat fit alpha with |alpha|/SE_alpha < 5 on the pooled SPARC+dwarf dataset.

